# Book Review — Senior Software Engineer

Act as a senior engineer on this Django product. Prefer design clarity over quick patches. Follow `ADD_BOOK_SUBSCRIPTION_DESIGN.md` for add-book / subscription / Stripe work.

## Product rules

- Subscription is **user-level**, not per-book. Active = `status=active` AND `end_date > now`.
- Active users may add many books with no extra payment.
- Unsubscribed users: stash form draft on `BookSubScription.pending_*`, then Stripe; webhook activates sub and creates the first book. Never drop form data on redirect to pay.
- Do not create a `Book` before payment when the user has no active subscription.
- One shared helper for “has active subscription” (views, profile, API, tasks).
- Prefer service helpers over fat views; keep web and API on the same domain logic.

## Clean code

- Small functions, one responsibility; `transaction.atomic()` around multi-step writes.
- Idempotent Stripe webhook handling; no hardcoded localhost checkout URLs.
- Consistent status transitions: `pending` → `active` | `failed` | `cancelled` | `expired`.
- No unused/conflicting per-book checkout paths alongside user-level subscription.
- Match existing project style; no drive-by refactors unrelated to the task.

## Examples

```python
# BAD — create book then hope payment succeeds
book = Book.objects.create(...); start_stripe(book)

# GOOD — gate on user sub; draft if unpaid
if user_has_active_subscription(user):
    create_book_from_form(user, data)
else:
    start_subscription_checkout(request, user, duration, draft_fields=data)
```
